# Sutram — ROUND-165: five transformations, attacked before they are built

**Base:** main at `b9dfba0c` (compiler v315) · **Code read:** v318 `c1f516f8`
(R173 mala hoist, staged) and v319 `4c1159be` (R175+R176, "a proven-local
kosh or mala is made once anywhere in a loop", staged on v318) ·
**Deliver into:** `Muse-to-Sarvam/`

**Read only. Nothing compiled or run.** All line numbers are
`src/sutram_compiler.asm` unless noted, at `4c1159be` (v319) unless marked
`c1f516f8` (v318) or `b9dfba0c` (the tip: the ROUND-165 and ROUND-177 briefs,
the R175 hand-back). Every decisive claim in this report was re-read against
the code by the parent after the four task notes were written; the
brief-correction counts (gate counts, guard corpus, differ-list) were
re-derived from the trees, not carried from a brief.

## Verdicts first

| Transformation | Verdict |
|---|---|
| A. Detach on escape | **Does not hold.** Two breaking programs, plus a design ambiguity that makes the brief's three-instruction sequence unimplementable as written. |
| B. Ping-pong for `curr = next` | **Not closed.** The recycled descriptor is not finished in three ways, and the stated shape cannot carry a value at all. |
| C. Move the zeroing from `mandapa` exit into `nirmmita` | **No.** The central premise ("a `mala` zeroes at creation") is false, the "below the block's mark" qualifier is unimplementable as written, and the guard claim is false — the DIFFER set grows from 38 to 404. |
| D. `madvise(MADV_DONTNEED)` threshold at block exit | **Sound, with three conditions** (page-alignment rounding, both cursor-raising routines, the threshold named as unmeasured and the 38 DIFFER guard cost disclosed). |
| E. Remove the compile-time-constant restriction on `kosh temp[n]` | **Sound but vacuous — the restriction does not exist.** The gate already asserts the runtime-capacity case is hoisted. |

## 0. The premise correction, before anything else: v319 does NOT contain the block-exit zeroing

All four tasks found this independently, and it changes what this round is
attacking. The R175 hand-back (commit `7c2535d0`, at the tip) describes the
block-exit zeroing as built and staged ("staged r175-nested-and-zero (SHA in
the follow-up commit)"), with evidence files (`Claude-to-Sarvam/evidence-r175/`
at the tip) and a pin change (`blind_kosh_via_call` 99 → 0). **The staged
branch does not contain it:**

- `git diff c1f516f8..4c1159be -- src/r65_arena.inc` is **0 lines**. The v319
  diff is the hoist (`r175_walk`, `r175_cand`, `r175_while_enter`, the R82
  generation stamp, the version bump, one new gate) plus version strings.
- The v319 commit message says so in so many words, line 17: "NOT in this
  branch (R176): zeroing a block's memory at its exits — staged separately, a
  decision not yet taken."
- There is no `r175_emit_zero_to_mark` anywhere in the tree, no
  `r175-nested-and-zero` branch on origin, and the pin still reads `'99'` at
  `4c1159be:tests/r73_scratch/test_scratch.py:30`.
- `r73_gen_rewind` at v319 (`src/r65_arena.inc:404-412`) is exactly `end =
  the mark` — `gen_expr` the mark, `mov [end],rax` — no zeroing, no `madvise`,
  no call of any kind.

So at v319, block exit is exactly as v315: **one cursor restore, no zeroing**.
The v319 branch's own guard line is **0 DIFFER** (587 byte-identical, 196
refused identically, ELF and PE, against v318 and v315 —
`evidence-r175/g1_318_elf-summary.txt`, `g1_318_pe-summary.txt`); the 38
DIFFER line belongs to step 2, the exit zeroing, measured against v315 and
against step 1 (`g2_315_elf-summary.txt`, `g2_step1_elf-summary.txt`: 549
byte-identical, 196 refused identically, 38 DIFFER, 0 new).

This bears on ROUND-177 WS2 and WS6, whose premise ("the zeroing change
broke the guard to 38, WS2 should restore it") refers to a build that is not
in the repo. The exit zeroing exists only as described in the hand-back and
measured in the evidence files; its code is not in any tree this round can
read. Task 3 therefore attacks a **proposal** (C), not a build — and the code
C would have to work with (the `kosh` read path, the `mala` creation path,
both `nirmmita` paths) is what decides it.

---

## 1. Task 1 — A: detach on escape. Does not hold.

The proposal (ROUND-165 §1.A / ROUND-177 WS1): stop proving non-escape by
walking the syntax tree. Emit the hoist unconditionally, and at the moment the
compiler emits an escape — `best = temp` where `best` was declared outside
the loop — emit one extra instruction sequence:

```
mov  rax, [rbp - temp_slot]
mov  [rbp - best_slot], rax      ; best now owns the descriptor
mov  QWORD [rbp - temp_slot], 0  ; DETACH
```

Next pass sees zero and creates a fresh descriptor; `best` keeps the old one.
The claim: "You need zero AST lookahead walk. Every `kosh` in every loop is
hoisted unconditionally. Even a `kosh` that conditionally escapes on 3 out of
800,000 passes reuses its memory on the other 799,997."

### 1.0 Ground truth: the slot mechanics (v319)

- **One slot per DECLARATION, not per name.** `r175_walk` (`:10918`) finds
  declarations anywhere in a loop (under a `yadi`/`anya`, in a `mandapa`, in
  a `yavat`); `r175_cand` (`:10959`) registers each one. Two declarations of
  the same name (a `yadi` and its `anya`) get **two slots**. The scan
  requirement changed from `r172_cnt == 1` (v317/v318) to `r172_cnt != 0`
  (the name must appear at least once).
- **Slot lookup is by DECL POINTER only.** `r172_slot_of` (`:11031`) scans
  `r172_decl` (256 entries) for the pointer and returns the frame-slot
  offset, or 0. There is NO name → slot map. The hidden var-table entries are
  all named `r172_hidden` (`"0k172"`, `:11028`), indistinguishable by name;
  `r7_find_var` returns the FIRST match. An `AST_VAR` node carries only
  `[type][name_ptr]` — no link to its declaration.
- **Two slots hold the descriptor.** After a declaration, BOTH the name's
  var-table slot and the hoist's hidden slot hold it: `.gs_decl_kosh`
  (`:19801`) allocates, `r172_decl_end` (`:11136`) emits `mov [rbp+slot],rax`
  (the hoist slot), then `alloc_var` + `emit_store_reg` stores it in the
  NAME's slot. On a reuse pass, `r172_decl_begin` (`:11051`) loads the hoist
  slot (`mov rcx,[rbp+slot]; test rcx,rcx; jz .fresh; mov qword [rcx-16],0` —
  length reset only) and the name's slot is refreshed from it.
- **The hoist slot is read only by `r172_decl_begin`** (at the declaration,
  i.e. at the top of the next pass). Every USE of a name loads from the NAME's
  slot, per use, with no register caching across statements:
  `emit_load_named_var` (`:29991`) → `lookup_var` → `mov rax,[rbp+offset]`.
- **The descriptor is a persistent allocation** (from cursor `cur`, which
  grows up, forward-only). The `mandapa` rewind (`r73_gen_rewind`,
  `src/r65_arena.inc:404`) restores only the scratch cursor `end`.
- **The scan (v319, `r172_scan` `:11151`) is name-based.** `.var` (`:11210`):
  any `AST_VAR` of the name → `.esc`. `.decl` (`:11263`): a same-kind
  re-declaration is tolerated and increments `r172_cnt`. `.args` (`:11325`)
  and `.builtin` (`:11293`) scan all args except a whitelisted arg0
  (builtins 37, 38, 39, 42, 44, 45; `kosh_ksham` 40 NOT whitelisted).

### 1.1 STRONGEST — two names for one loop-local kosh (alias escape)

```
kosh best[1]
chakra j = 1 tak 800000 {
    kosh temp[10]
    temp[0] = j
    vitti b = temp
    best = b
}
likha(best[0], "\n")
```

Note on the brief's form: "`kosh b = temp`" is not an alias form — a kosh has
no initializer (scan comment `:11289`; `.gs_decl_kosh` ignores the
initializer slot). The alias form is `vitti b = temp` (`.gs_decl` `:19728`
calls `r38_mark_alias` when the initializer is an `AST_VAR`). The break
stands with the `vitti` form. A second name created by assignment (`b =
temp`, with `b` declared earlier) is the same as by declaration: both copy
the descriptor into `b`'s name slot, and neither creates a hoist slot for
`b`.

**Why no detach fires.** The detach design fires on an escape — an assignment
whose value is a hoisted kosh, to a name outside the loop. Neither assignment
qualifies:

- `vitti b = temp` — the value IS `temp`, but `b` is loop-local, so it is not
  an escape. Correctly no detach.
- `best = b` — the value is `b`, not a hoisted declaration. The detach
  emitter sees the name `b` and has no way to know `b` holds `temp`'s
  descriptor.

**Why the emitter cannot see through the alias.**

- `r38_mark_alias` (`:28286`) copies only the KIND to the target name (via
  the `is_kosh_var` / `is_array_var` / `r23_is_slice_var` name lists). The
  SOURCE is not recorded.
- `r108_note_alias` (`:13202`) records (node, target name, depth) in the
  `r108_bk` tables — but only for koshs made in an open `mandapa` (it returns
  immediately if `r108_bk_n == 0`, and `r108_bk_of` on the value returns 0 for
  a non-mandapa kosh). A loop-local kosh NOT in a `mandapa` is never in the
  `r108_bk` table, so its aliases are recorded NOWHERE.
- `r126_check_assign` (`:29751`) `.local` allows a kosh→kosh local assignment
  via `r126_bind` (kind match). It does not distinguish "best outside the
  loop" from "best inside the loop" — there is no `decl_depth` (R161: it does
  not exist; only `r73_var_depth`, mandapa depth only).

**Trace, pass by pass.**

- Pass 1: `temp` declared; hoist slot S = D1 (fresh descriptor).
  `temp[0] = 1`. `vitti b = temp`: `b`'s name slot = D1. `best = b`:
  `best`'s name slot = D1. No detach fires.
- Pass 2: `temp` declared; S still holds D1 (never detached) → reuse:
  length reset to 0, `temp[0] = 2`. But `best` ALSO holds D1, so `best[0]`
  is now 2, not 1. **Aliasing — wrong value.**
- Pass 800,000: `best` holds D1 and `best[0]` = 800,000. The final
  `likha(best[0])` prints **800,000 instead of 1**.

**What it would take to close it.** Detach on EVERY copy of a hoisted
descriptor (including `vitti b = temp`), i.e. detach on every assignment
whose value holds a hoisted kosh — which requires the alias tracking the
design does not have, and which undercuts the performance claim (a kosh
passed to a read-only use would be remade every pass). That is a different
design from "detach on escape". In v319 this program is safe, but only
because the scan escapes the NAME (`temp` appears as an `AST_VAR` in `vitti
b = temp`, `.var` → `.esc`), so `temp` is not hoisted at all — the break is
specific to the unconditional-hoist detach design.

### 1.2 Two declarations, one name (the anya-branch escape)

```
kosh best[1]
chakra j = 1 tak 800000 {
    yadi (j % 2 == 0) {
        kosh temp[10]
        temp[0] = j
    } anya {
        kosh temp[20]
        temp[0] = j
        best = temp
    }
}
```

Two hoist slots: A (the `yadi` branch) and B (the `anya` branch). The escape
`best = temp` is in the `anya` branch (declaration B). A name-based detach
resolves to the FIRST slot in source order (A) — `r7_find_var` returns the
first match, and the hidden entries are all named `"0k172"` — while the escape
is in branch B. Slot B is never detached, so the next `anya` pass reuses B's
descriptor while `best` holds it. **Aliasing.** Slot A is detached
needlessly, so the next `yadi` pass makes a fresh descriptor for A (a leak in
the "memory not reused" sense — the old A descriptor is abandoned in the
arena and still counted by `parimana()`).

Detaching ALL slots for the name would close it, but that is not the design
as described (singular "temp_slot"), and there is no name → slots map to
detach from — the only lookup is by decl pointer (`r172_slot_of` `:11031`),
and a use site carries no decl pointer (an `AST_VAR` carries only
`[type][name_ptr]`). In v319 this program is safe (the scan escapes the NAME,
so neither declaration is hoisted) — the break is specific to the
unconditional-hoist detach design.

### 1.3 The two-slot ambiguity — the brief's "temp_slot" does not exist

The brief's sequence names ONE slot. There are TWO slots holding `temp`'s
descriptor (§1.0). The three single-slot readings:

- **"temp_slot" = the NAME's slot.** The sequence IS the assignment (`best`
  gets the descriptor, `temp`'s name slot is zeroed). Then a later same-pass
  `likha(temp[0])` loads `temp`'s descriptor from the name slot
  (`emit_load_named_var` `:29991` reloads the slot per use) → loads 0 → the
  index reads `[0-16]` for the length (bounds check) → **null deref,
  crash**. AND the detach fails its purpose: the hoist slot still holds the
  descriptor, so the next pass reuses it while `best` holds it → **aliasing**.
  Double break.
- **"temp_slot" = the HOIST slot.** The copy is redundant (the normal
  assignment already stored the descriptor into `best`'s slot), and the zero
  is the actual detach. Same-pass reads of `temp` work (the name slot still
  holds the descriptor). Next pass is fresh. **Correct for this case.**
- **Both slots zeroed.** The detach works, but same-pass reads of `temp`
  crash (name slot is 0). Break.

So the design must zero exactly the hoist slot and leave the name slot alone.
The brief's sequence as written is ambiguous, and its literal reading
(`temp_slot` = the name's slot, since that is the slot the assignment uses)
is wrong on both counts. The design as described is not implementable as
written — it needs the two-slot distinction spelled out.

The same ambiguity decides the brief's case 2 (a detach inside a branch that
runs twice) and case 8d below: under the "detach is extra, zero the hoist
slot after the normal assignment" reading, a second detach on an
already-zero slot is harmless; under the "detach replaces the assignment"
reading, the second detach copies 0 into the target (null descriptor).

### 1.4 Call escape — the FFI path is a missing hook (conditional break)

The escape is a call, not an assignment. Where the detach fires:

- **Typed kosh parameter.** The shared call-arg codegen `gen_typed_call_arg`
  (`.gtca_kosh`) loads the argument and pushes it; no `r126`/`r108` check is
  called there. The "keeps" information (`r108_param_kept` fixpoint) is
  computed by `r108_check_call`, which runs ONLY on the expression path
  (`.ge_funcall` `:22081`, call site `:22084`). On the statement path
  (`.gs_funcall` `:19550`) the fixpoint is never computed. So a detach
  emitter in `.gtca_kosh` cannot know whether the callee keeps the kosh.
  Detaching on EVERY kosh-parameter call is CORRECT (fail-closed: the callee
  gets the descriptor in a register before the detach zeroes the hoist slot;
  a non-keeping call just forces a fresh descriptor next pass) but is a
  performance loss, not a correctness break.
- **Foreign call.** `.gs_funcall` dispatches to `r33_gen_foreign_funcall`
  BEFORE the typed-arg path and before `r129`/`r25`/`r108` — and
  `r108_check_call` never runs on this path. The FFI door affirmatively
  permits a kosh by its own name to a `ptr` parameter
  (`src/r32_ffi.inc:688-699`, `.ptr` `:736-755`: `mov rax,[rax]` hands C the
  elements address). A detach in `.gtca_kosh` does NOT fire for an FFI call
  — the FFI path bypasses `gen_typed_call_arg` entirely. The detach emitter
  needs a SEPARATE hook in the FFI dispatch (both `.gs_funcall` and
  `.ge_funcall`). For the FFI, "keeps" is unanswerable (C can keep the
  pointer indefinitely), so the detach must fire on EVERY foreign call
  passing a hoisted kosh to a `ptr` parameter. **If the hook is missed, the
  break is real:** C keeps the elements address, the next pass reuses the
  descriptor, and on growth the elements move / the old region is
  `munmap`'d, so C's pointer dangles → stale read or crash.

Ranked below §1.1–1.3 because the fix (detach on every kosh-parameter call,
plus hook the FFI dispatch) is well-defined — but the design as described
names only the assignment form, so the FFI hook is a genuine gap.

### 1.5 Store in an outer kosh — closed today, conditional on the refusal

`kosh_kship(outer, temp)`: `r126_check_builtin` (`:29807`) refuses a kosh at
arg index 1 (`r134_holds_kosh` `:28923`, kinds 2/3/6; index ≠ 0 → `.bad`).
**This program does not compile today**, so the detach is unnecessary here
— closed by the refusal, not by the detach.

If the refusal is removed (because the detach "makes it unnecessary"), the
detach must fire at the `kosh_kship` call — and `kosh_kship` is a BUILTIN
(`AST_CALL`), whose codegen is different from `.gs_funcall`, so the emitter
needs a hook in the builtin call path too. If the refusal is removed but the
detach is missed, the next pass reuses `temp`'s descriptor while `outer`'s
element holds it → aliasing. The detach CAN handle it if hooked (the element
keeps the old descriptor; the next pass is fresh) — but the design must say
which it intends: refusal stays, or refusal goes and the detach is hooked.

### 1.6 Cases closed by the design itself

- **Detach inside a branch that runs twice** (brief case 2): `yadi (c)
  { best = temp }` on pass 5 and pass 500. Under the "detach is extra"
  reading: pass 5 detaches (`best` = D5, hoist slot = 0); pass 6 makes a
  fresh D6; pass 500 detaches D500 into `best`; pass 501 makes a fresh D501.
  `best` holds D500. Correct. (A branch runs at most once per pass, so the
  hoist slot is non-zero at each detach.) The discriminating case is §1.7.
- **Escape twice to two outer names** (case 8d): `best1 = temp;
  best2 = temp`. Under the "detach is extra" reading, the first detach
  zeroes the hoist slot and the second zeroes an already-zero slot
  (harmless); `best1` and `best2` both hold the descriptor (both got it from
  the normal assignments); the next pass is fresh. **Closed — but only under
  the "detach is extra" reading.** Under the "detach replaces the
  assignment" reading, the second detach copies 0 (the hoist slot is already
  zero) into `best2` → null descriptor → wrong value.
- **Escape on the last pass only** (case 8e): the detach fires, the loop
  ends, `best` holds the descriptor. The loop-exit path does nothing to the
  hoist slot (it is a stack slot in the function's frame). Correct.
- **Recursion** (case 6): closed. The frame slot is per-frame
  (`rbp`-relative); each recursive invocation has its own frame and its own
  hoist slot at the same compile-time offset. The `r172_slot` table is a
  per-function compile-time map (decl pointer → slot offset), not a holder
  of runtime values; `var_cnt` resets per function (`:18585`).
  `r172_for_enter` zeroes the slot at the loop's entry in the current frame.
  A recursive call inside the loop gets a fresh frame with a fresh slot. No
  interaction.
- **Detach inside a `mandapa`** (case 7, the brief's first check): closed on
  the brief's question. The descriptor is a persistent allocation (from
  `cur`); the `mandapa` rewind (`r73_gen_rewind`,
  `src/r65_arena.inc:404`) restores only the scratch cursor `end`, so the
  detached descriptor's buffer survives the rewind. The hoist's frame slot is
  NOT touched by the block's exit: `r82_block_leave` (`:10825`) zeroes the
  block's own R82 slot (`r82_emit_zero` `:10804`) and sets
  `r82_off[gdepth] = 0` — a different stack slot (allocated from the same
  `stack_dep` counter but a different offset). So a detach inside a `mandapa`
  persists: the slot is still zero on the next pass, and the next pass creates
  a fresh descriptor.
  - R82 note: a detached descriptor carries the block's generation at
    `[d-24]` (written by the R175 stamp on reuse, or by
    `r82_emit_kosh_header` at creation). After the block exits, the block's
    liveness slot is zeroed, so if `best` is later passed to a `prakriya`,
    `r82_emit_check` (`:11531`) reads `[rcx]` = 0 ≠ `[d-24]` → STALE → the
    `prakriya` refuses it. That is CORRECT (the kosh escaped its block; the
    check catches it) — the detach does not defeat the R82 liveness check —
    but it means a detached `mandapa`-kosh handed to a `prakriya` is refused
    at runtime, which the design should say is intended.
- **Return escape** (case 8a): `nivartanam temp`. `.gs_return` (`:21483`):
  `r126_check_return`, `r23_check_return`, `gen_expr` (value into `rax`),
  `r73_gen_ret_rewind` (`src/r65_arena.inc:535`, `rax` kept), then the
  epilogue (`ret`). A detach after `gen_expr` would zero the hoist slot in
  the CURRENT frame — but the frame is about to be destroyed, so the detach
  is meaningless and unnecessary (there is no next pass; the function
  returns). A future invocation gets a fresh frame with a fresh slot (zeroed
  at loop entry). Closed. (`r126_check_return`'s `phal` branch refuses a kosh
  value outright; the plain branch allows a kosh-returning `prakriya` to
  return a kosh.)

### 1.7 Cases closed only by a pre-existing refusal (the detach does not help)

- **Slice escape** (case 8b): a slice of a hoisted kosh stored outside the
  loop (`vitti s = k[0:2]`, or `kosh_kship(outer, k[0:2])`). The slice holds
  the ELEMENTS address directly. The detach (zeroing the hoist slot) does NOT
  invalidate a stored slice — the slice still points at the old elements. If
  the kosh grows, the elements move and the old region is `munmap`'d, so the
  stored slice points at unmapped memory → crash on read. The detach does NOT
  make slice-keeping safe. The slice refusal must stay (`r134_holds_kosh`
  covers slices via `r134_kosh_slice`; `r23_check_return` refuses a slice
  return; E_KOSH_VIBHAG). If the doors are removed, the slice escape breaks.
- **Function-value escape** (case 8c): a kosh passed through a variable
  holding the `prakriya`. `gen_typed_call_arg`'s scalar path calls
  `r126_check_untyped_arg` (`:29871`), which for an indirect call (`.known`)
  still checks `r134_holds_kosh` on the argument and refuses
  (`r126_d_par`); `r108_check_call` refuses an unknown callee on the
  expression path. The program does not compile. Closed by the refusal.
- **The `mala` (open question, not a proven break).** The detach is described
  for a kosh (a descriptor). A `mala` has no descriptor — its "descriptor" is
  a frame address — and the design does not say whether the detach extends to
  it. This matters because, for a `mala`, the scan is the ONLY net (R164: no
  pre-existing door refuses a `mala` anywhere), so under the detach design (no
  scan) a `mala` escape is handled ONLY by the detach. If the detach does not
  cover malas, or covers them wrongly, there is no fallback. Not verified
  (the `r173_mala_begin` fresh path was not traced in full); listed in §6.

### 1.8 Design questions (ROUND-177 WS1)

**(1) Does the detach break the current escape checks — can they stay, or
does the detach make them unnecessary (and should they go or stay)?**

The detach does NOT break the checks (it operates on the hoist slot; the
checks operate on kinds/names at compile time). The checks CAN stay.

The detach does NOT make them unnecessary, and **they should stay**. The
checks close escape paths the detach cannot see or cannot fix:

- **Slices** (§1.7): the detach does not invalidate a stored slice; the
  refusal must stay, or a stored slice dangles after growth.
- **The FFI door** (§1.4): the detach must fire in the FFI dispatch; the
  door's refusal of a kosh to `i64`/`f64` parameters must stay (the detach
  cannot fix a type mismatch).
- **The `mala`** (§1.7): the scan is the only net; if the detach does not
  cover malas, nothing does.
- **The function-value and `kosh_kship` refusals** (§1.5, §1.7): they must
  stay unless the detach is hooked into those paths.

The "zero AST lookahead walk" claim is about the HOIST scan (`r172_scan`),
not about the `r126`/`r108`/`r134` doors — those are per-site checks, not
lookahead walks. So the detach can remove the scan but must keep the doors.
The detach is a complement to the checks (it handles the assignment escape
for a kosh), not a replacement.

**(2) Does the detach make the hoist unconditional, so the nested-block work
in R175 becomes the only rule?**

No — the detach does NOT subsume the nested-block work. The two are
independent, and the detach DEPENDS on it.

The v319 diff (`c1f516f8..4c1159be`, 27 files; compiler
`src/sutram_compiler.asm` +154 lines) is the nested-block work: `r175_walk`
(`:10918`) finds declarations anywhere in a loop (under a `yadi`/`anya`, in
a `mandapa`, in a `yavat`); `r175_cand` (`:10959`) registers each one; the
scan requirement changed from `r172_cnt == 1` to `r172_cnt != 0`; a
declaration that never runs makes nothing; a kosh reused inside a `mandapa`
gets this pass's block generation in its header (the R175 stamp). New test
`tests/r175/test_nested_made_once.py` (185 lines); new gate "R175 nested
made once" in `tools/gates.sh`.

- The nested-block work answers WHERE a declaration can be hoisted (anywhere
  in a loop, one slot per DECLARATION). The detach answers WHAT HAPPENS when
  a hoisted kosh escapes (detach its slot so the next pass is fresh).
- The detach does not remove the need for per-declaration slots — §1.2 shows
  the detach needs to know WHICH declaration's slot to detach, and there are
  two, one per branch. The nested-block work's per-declaration slot table
  (`r172_decl`/`r172_slot`) is exactly the structure the detach needs to
  find the slot. Without it, the detach has no slot to zero for a
  branch-local declaration.
- Conversely, the nested-block work does not remove the need for the detach
  (it still relies on the scan to refuse escapes; the detach replaces the
  scan's refusal with a runtime detach).

So: the detach makes the hoist unconditional in the sense that it removes the
SCAN (the proof), but the nested-block work (per-declaration slots, the walk
that finds declarations anywhere in the loop) remains the rule for WHERE the
hoist applies — and the detach depends on it. The two are independent
mechanisms that compose: R175 says "hoist every declaration anywhere in the
loop"; the detach says "when one escapes, detach its slot".

**Ranked breaking programs for A (strongest first):**

1. **§1.1 — two names for one loop-local kosh** (`vitti b = temp` then
   `best = b`): breaks even the best-form design. No detach fires; the next
   pass reuses the descriptor `best` holds. Wrong value (final
   `likha(best[0])` prints 800,000 instead of 1). The detach cannot see
   through an alias, and no alias tracking records the source.
2. **§1.2 — two declarations, one name** (the `anya`-branch escape): breaks
   the best-form design. A name-based detach resolves to the wrong slot (the
   `yadi` branch's), so the `anya` branch's slot is never detached. Aliasing,
   plus a leak of the wrongly-detached slot.
3. **§1.3 — the two-slot ambiguity**: the brief's "temp_slot" does not exist
   as a single slot. The literal reading (zero the name's slot) is a double
   break (same-pass read of `temp` crashes; the detach fails).
4. **§1.4 — the FFI call path**: conditional break. If the detach is not
   hooked into `r33_gen_foreign_funcall`, C keeps a dangling elements address
   after growth.
5. **§1.5 — `kosh_kship` into an outer kosh**: conditional break. Closed
   today by the refusal; breaks only if the refusal is removed without
   hooking the builtin path.

---

## 2. Task 2 — B: ping-pong for loop-carried `curr = next`. Not closed.

The proposal (ROUND-165 §1.B / ROUND-177 WS1 question 2): instead of
detaching, recycle — write the old `curr` descriptor into `next`'s slot.
Claim: two descriptors for 800,000 passes. The loop-carried pattern is: each
pass, `curr` holds the previous pass's value and `next` is built fresh, then
`curr = next` at the end of the pass (so the next pass's `curr` is this pass's
`next`).

### 2.0 The slot states, and what `curr = next` does today

A `kosh` variable's stack slot holds the **descriptor pointer** (8 bytes) —
the address of `[d]`, the elements word. The descriptor is four qwords:
`[d-16]` length, `[d-8]` capacity, `[d]` elements address, `[d+8]` region
size (32 bytes; 56 with the R82 header).

- **Load** (`.ge_var`, `:25839`): `mov rax,[rbp+offset]` — the descriptor
  pointer.
- **Store** (`.gs_assign` generic path, `:20019`): `gen_expr` of the value,
  then store `rax` into the target's slot. So `curr = next` today copies the
  8-byte pointer D2 from `next`'s slot into `curr`'s slot. After it, **both
  names alias the same descriptor D2**; `next`'s slot still holds D2 too.
- **Decl** (`.gs_decl_kosh`, `:19802`): evaluates the capacity, calls
  `r172_decl_begin` (reuse path: `mov rcx,[rbp+slot]; test rcx,rcx;
  jz .fresh; mov qword [rcx-16],0; mov rax,rcx; jmp .have`), otherwise
  allocates via `r108_kosh_alloc`, then `r172_decl_end` (`mov [rbp+slot],rax`
  — the hoist slot) and `alloc_var` + `emit_store_reg` (the variable's own
  slot). **Both slots get the descriptor pointer, and both are refreshed from
  it every pass.** The hoist slot is zeroed once at loop entry (`r175_cand`
  emits `mov qword [rbp+slot],0`).
- **Read** (`r127_ix`, `:577-578`): `mov rcx,[rax-16]` (length);
  `mov rax,[rax]` (elements); `mov [rsp],rax; push rcx`. Then
  `emit_array_bounds_check` (`:21658`): `cmp rax,[rsp]` (index vs the length
  freshly read from the descriptor); `jb .ok` — otherwise the diagnostic and
  exit 1. **A kosh read never looks past `length`.** Stale elements beyond the
  length are invisible; a read of a length-0 kosh always refuses. (This
  confirms the Task 3 premise for `kosh`, and it is what makes the recycled
  descriptor safe *once its length is reset* — and unsafe before that.)
- **Growth** (`r127_emit_grow`, `src/r65_arena.inc:579`): operates entirely
  through the descriptor pointer in `r8` — `[r8+8]` region size, `[r8-8]`
  capacity, `[r8]` elements. If the new size fits the region it stays in
  place; otherwise it allocates a new buffer, copies element-by-element, then
  releases the old region (`mov rdi,rsi; mov rsi,rcx; mov eax,11; syscall` —
  munmap) and writes `[r8]`, `[r8-8]`, `[r8+8]` together. Every name holding
  the descriptor pointer sees the new buffer, because no name holds the
  elements pointer directly.

The ping-pong as stated: at `curr = next`, instead of only copying D2 into
`curr`'s slot, ALSO write D1 (the old `curr` descriptor) into `next`'s slot.
Two descriptors for 800,000 passes: they alternate between the two names.

### 2.1 What the ping-pong needs, and why it is not a small emitter change

**The scan currently refuses to hoist either name in the loop-carried
pattern.** `r172_scan`'s own comment (`:11187`) says it plainly:
`AST_ASSIGN ; the target: a VAR (the name itself escapes) or an INDEX`. The
`.two_8_16` handler (`:11224`) scans `[rbx+8]` (target) and `[rbx+16]`
(value). For the `curr` candidate, the target `AST_VAR(curr)` → `.var` →
`r172_is_name` matches → `.esc`. For the `next` candidate, the value
`AST_VAR(next)` → `.var` → `.esc`. **So under the current scan, a loop
containing `curr = next` hoists NEITHER name — both are made fresh every
pass, and the ping-pong never fires.** The proposal therefore needs a scan
change (whitelist this assignment as a *transfer*, not an escape) as well as
an emitter change (the swap). The proposal describes neither.

**And the scan cannot express the rule the ping-pong needs.** The rule is
PAIRWISE: *this specific assignment, between these two specific names, is a
transfer; every other assignment of these names is an escape.* But the scan
is per-name — it walks the loop once per candidate and sees uses of ONE name
at a time (`r172_is_name` compares one string). It has no notion of a pair,
and no state that survives from one candidate's scan to the next. The emitter
sees one assignment at a time and has no notion of a pair either. The alias
machinery does not help: `r38_mark_alias` (`:28286`) records only the KIND
(a target of a kosh assignment is marked a kosh), and `r108_note_alias`
(`:13202`) records (node, name) pairs for block-kosh tracking — **neither
records which descriptor a name holds at a given point.** Tracking that
would need a dataflow analysis the compiler does not have.

So any implementation must approximate the pair notion, and every
approximation breaks on a concrete program (§2.4, §2.5). The breaks below are
stated per approximation where they differ.

**The two implementation shapes.** (A) *Emitter swap:* at every kosh-to-kosh
assignment whose right-hand side is hoisted, emit the swap instead of the
plain copy. (B) *Scan whitelist:* the scan whitelists the loop-carried
assignment (by some rule), the name is hoisted, and the emitter swaps only at
that assignment. The proposal does not say which. The strongest breaks (P3,
P1) hit both shapes; P2a hits shape B's natural approximation, P2b hits
shape A.

### 2.2 Case 1 — `curr` is read after the swap; `next` is read after the swap

**`curr` after the swap: fine.** `curr`'s slot holds D2 (this pass's `next`),
so `curr[i]` reads this pass's values — the same as plain semantics.

**`next` after the swap: BROKEN.** `next`'s slot holds D1 — the OLD `curr`
descriptor, with its old length and its old elements. The length is reset
only at the NEXT pass's decl (`r172_decl_begin`); between the swap and that
decl, `next` is observable with D1's stale state. The program:

```sm
mukhya() {
    kosh curr[2]
    chakra i = 1 tak 2 {
        kosh next[2]
        kosh_kship(next, i * 10)
        curr = next
        yadi i == 2 { likha(next[0]) }
    }
}
```

Trace (outside reading, `curr` outside the loop, `next` hoisted):

- Pass 1: `curr` = D1 (empty, from the outer decl). `next` decl → fresh D2.
  `next` = [10]. Swap: `curr` ← D2 ([10]), `next` ← D1 (empty, length 0).
- Pass 2: `next` decl → slot holds D1 → reset `[D1-16]` = 0 → `next` is D1,
  empty. `next` = [20] (D1). Swap: `curr` ← D1 ([20]), `next` ← D2
  ([10], length 1). `likha(next[0])` → D2[0] = **10**.
- Plain semantics: after `curr = next`, `next` still holds D2 — on pass 2
  that is the descriptor built this pass ([20]) — so it prints **20**.

**Stale read: 10 instead of 20.** The recycled descriptor's old length (1)
makes its old elements readable, because the kosh read bounds-checks against
the length *currently in the descriptor* — and the swap does not reset it.

Without the `yadi` guard the break is louder: on pass 1, `likha(next[0])`
after the swap reads D1, which is empty (length 0) → the bounds check fails
→ **exit 1 instead of printing 10**.

**Mitigation that does not work:** reset D1's length to 0 at the swap
(`mov qword [rax-16],0` after the moves). Then `next[0]` after the swap fails
the bounds check (exit 1) instead of printing a stale value — still different
from plain semantics (which prints D2's value). And the reset introduces a
new crash on the zero case (§2.6): if `curr`'s slot is 0, the reset writes
through address −16 → segfault. The only correct mitigation is to prove
`next` is never read after the swap — which is an ordering property the scan
does not track (it walks the AST in order but records no positions, and the
proposal does not add any).

### 2.3 Case 3 — three names: the strongest break (P3)

**The ping-pong does not know about the third name.** `prev = curr` before
the swap makes `prev` hold D1. The swap recycles D1 into `next`'s slot. The
next pass writes into D1 through `next` — and `prev` still points at it. The
program:

```sm
mukhya() {
    kosh curr[2]
    kosh prev[2]
    chakra i = 1 tak 3 {
        kosh next[2]
        kosh_kship(next, i * 10)
        yadi i == 1 { prev = curr }
        curr = next
        yadi i == 3 { likha(prev[0]) }
    }
}
```

Trace, plain semantics (current v319, no ping-pong — `next` is NOT hoisted,
because the scan flags `curr = next`):

- Pass 1: `curr` = D1 (empty). `next` = fresh D2 = [10]. `prev = curr` →
  `prev` ← D1 (empty, length 0). `curr = next` (plain copy) → `curr` ← D2.
- Pass 2: `next` = fresh D3 = [20]. `curr = next` → `curr` ← D3.
- Pass 3: `next` = fresh D4 = [30]. `curr = next` → `curr` ← D4.
  `likha(prev[0])`: `prev` holds D1, length 0 → bounds check fails → **exit 1**.

Trace, with ping-pong (scan whitelists `curr = next`, `next` hoisted):

- Pass 1: `curr` = D1 (empty). `next` decl → fresh D2 = [10]. `prev = curr`
  → `prev` ← D1 (empty). Swap: `curr` ← D2 ([10]), `next` ← D1.
- Pass 2: `next` decl → slot holds D1 → reset `[D1-16]` = 0 → `next` is D1,
  empty. `next` = [20] (D1). Swap: `curr` ← D1 ([20]), `next` ← D2.
- Pass 3: `next` decl → slot holds D2 → reset → `next` is D2, empty.
  `next` = [30] (D2). Swap: `curr` ← D2 ([30]), `next` ← D1.
  `likha(prev[0])`: `prev` holds D1 = **[20]** (built in pass 2, length 1)
  → prints **20**.

**Plain semantics exits 1; ping-pong prints 20.** The recycled descriptor
D1 was written (as `next` in pass 2) while `prev` still held it. `prev` was
assigned the *empty* `curr` of pass 1, and it now reads the *values* of pass
2's `next` — a wrong value, and the wrong outcome class (a value where there
should be a refusal).

Why the ping-pong cannot see it: the scan for the `next` candidate looks only
at uses of `next`. `prev = curr` does not mention `next`, so it cannot affect
the scan — and it must not, because `prev = curr` is a legitimate program
that compiles today. The alias machinery records that `prev` is a kosh
(`r38_mark_alias`), but not that `prev` holds D1, and not that D1 is the
descriptor about to be recycled. `r108_note_alias` records (node, name)
pairs, not descriptor identities. **There is no table in the compiler that
says "D1 is held by `prev`", so no emitter check can consult one.**

The same break in the other two third-name shapes:

- `saved = next` AFTER the swap: `saved` copies D1 (the recycled descriptor)
  from `next`'s slot; the next pass overwrites D1 through `next`, corrupting
  `saved`. (This is §2.5's case-2-after, with `saved` in place of `best`.)
- `vitti x = curr`: `x` is marked a kosh by `r38_mark_alias` (a plain-variable
  alias of a kosh marks the target the same kind), and holds D1 exactly as
  `prev` does — same trace, same break. (Note: `vitti x = curr` where `curr`
  is a kosh compiles because the kind is recorded, not refused — the kind
  rule refuses a kind *change* on an existing name, not a name *born* a
  kosh; R164 Task 2.)

### 2.4 Case 2 — `next` escapes before the swap

**Before the swap: CLOSED, if the scan gates the swap.** Any escape of `next`
— given to a `prakriya` (`.args` → `.esc`), stored in an outer `kosh`
(`kosh_kship` arg1 → `.esc`), returned (`.one_8` → `.esc`), assigned to an
outer name (`.two_8_16` → `.esc`) — flags `next` in the scan, so `next` is
not hoisted and the swap never fires. The escaped name holds D2, the next
pass makes a fresh `next`, and nothing aliases. (The swap itself would also
be safe here: after the swap `next`'s slot holds D1, the escaped name holds
D2, and the next pass writes only into D1 — but the swap never fires, so the
point is moot.)

**After the swap: OPEN — and it is the same break as §2.3, through a different
name.** `best = next` after `curr = next` copies the RECYCLED descriptor
D1 out of `next`'s slot, and the next pass overwrites it. The program:

```sm
mukhya() {
    kosh curr[2]
    kosh best[2]
    chakra i = 1 tak 2 {
        kosh next[2]
        kosh_kship(next, i * 10)
        curr = next
        best = next
        yadi i == 2 { likha(best[0]) }
    }
}
```

Trace, plain semantics: pass 2 prints **20** (`best` = this pass's `next`).
Trace, with ping-pong under approximation "whitelist assignments whose target
is declared outside the loop" (the natural scan rule — `best` is outside, so
`best = next` is whitelisted too, `next` is hoisted): pass 1, swap →
`curr` ← D2 ([10]), `next` ← D1; `best = next` (plain copy) → `best` ← D1
(empty). Pass 2: `next` = [20] (D1); swap → `curr` ← D1 ([20]), `next` ←
D2 ([10]); `best = next` → `best` ← D2 ([10]). Prints **10** instead of
**20** — and a third pass would reset D2 and overwrite `best`'s values.

This break is shape-dependent: it needs the scan to whitelist `best = next`
as well as `curr = next`. If the scan whitelists ONLY `curr = next` (by
target name), then `best = next` flags `next` → not hoisted → no swap →
prints 20. But whitelisting by target name requires designating `curr` as
the carried name — a new mechanism the proposal does not describe, and one
that still leaves §2.2 and §2.3 open (a read of `next` after the swap, and a
third name, do not depend on how the transfer is designated).

**Two swaps in one pass (shape A only).** Under the emitter-swap shape
(swap at EVERY kosh-to-kosh assignment whose right-hand side is hoisted),
`best = next; curr = next` in one pass inverts itself:

```sm
mukhya() {
    kosh curr[2]
    kosh best[2]
    chakra i = 1 tak 2 {
        kosh next[2]
        kosh_kship(next, i * 10)
        best = next
        curr = next
        likha(curr[0])
    }
}
```

Pass 1: `next` = D2 = [10]. `best = next` (swap) → `best` ← D2, `next` ←
D1 (empty). `curr = next` (swap) → `curr` ← D1 (EMPTY), `next` ← D2.
`likha(curr[0])` → D1 length 0 → **exit 1** (plain: prints 10). The second
swap undoes the first for `curr` and hands `next`'s slot back the descriptor
`best` holds. A swap is not idempotent and not composable: it assumes the
assignment is the ONLY kosh-to-kosh assignment involving these names in the
pass, exactly once, at the end. Under shape B (swap only at the whitelisted
`curr = next`), `best = next` is a plain copy and this program prints 10 —
so this break is specific to shape A. But shape A is the shape the detach
(§1) uses — the emitter fires at the escape site, without a scan — and the
proposal says "instead of detaching it", which reads as the same shape.

### 2.5 Case 4 — a pass where `next` is never written: CLOSED

```sm
mukhya() {
    kosh curr[2]
    chakra i = 1 tak 2 {
        kosh next[2]
        yadi i == 2 { kosh_kship(next, 20) }
        curr = next
    }
    likha(kosh_gan(curr))
}
```

Pass 1: `next` is declared, reset to length 0, never written. Swap:
`curr` ← D2 (empty, capacity 2), `next` ← D1 (empty, capacity 2). `curr`
after the swap is empty — correct, exactly as plain semantics (a plain copy
of an empty `next`). Pass 2: `next`'s decl resets D1 (length 0, capacity 2
preserved — the grown buffer is NOT lost; the capacity travels with the
descriptor). `next` = [20]. Swap: `curr` ← D1 ([20]), `next` ← D2.
`kosh_gan(curr)` = 1. Correct. The capacities are preserved because the swap
moves whole descriptors, and the growth path (§2.0) grows through the
descriptor, not through the slot. The only wrinkle is §2.2 again: a read of
`next` after the swap on the empty pass sees D1's old elements (with D1's old
length) rather than an empty `kosh`.

### 2.6 Case 5 — the first pass: `curr`'s slot is zero: CLOSED (for the plain swap)

When is `curr`'s slot zero at the swap? Not on a normal first pass — the decl
allocates D1 and stores it before the swap. It is zero only if `curr`'s decl
did not run this pass: with v319, a declaration under a `yadi` / `anya` or in
a `mandapa` inside the loop gets its own slot, and "a declaration that never
runs never makes anything" (R175 commit message). Trace: `curr`'s slot = 0,
`next`'s slot = D2. Swap: `curr` ← D2, `next` ← 0. After the swap, `curr`
holds D2 (correct — `curr = next`), `next` holds nothing. Next pass,
`next`'s decl sees slot 0 → fresh allocation D3. **Correct, but wasteful** —
the swap bought nothing this pass (D1 was never allocated), and it is exactly
what the detach would have written (0). No garbage is possible for a hoist
slot (zeroed at loop entry) or for a declared var slot (it holds the last
pass's descriptor, or the decl just stored one). The zero case is closed.

**But it closes the length-resetting mitigation.** If the swap also resets
the recycled descriptor's length (`mov qword [rax-16],0` with `rax` = the old
`curr`), then on the zero pass `rax` = 0 and the reset writes through address
−16 → **segfault**. So the two mitigations conflict: resetting the length
makes §2.2 fail loudly instead of silently, and crashes §2.6. A correct
implementation would need `test rax,rax; jz` around the reset — more
instructions (§2.8) — and it still would not fix §2.2's semantic difference
(refusal vs. value) or §2.3 at all.

### 2.7 Case 7 — the reset, and the both-inside reading

**The swap happens at the assignment (end of the pass); the reset happens at
the decl (top of the next pass).** `r172_decl_begin` runs when the decl
executes; the swap runs when `curr = next` executes. For `next`, the reset
zeroes the length of the RECYCLED descriptor D1 — correct, that is the point:
D1 is emptied for reuse. For `curr`, in the both-inside reading, the reset
zeroes the length of the CARRIED descriptor D2 — wrong, it destroys the
carried value. **The reset zeroes the right descriptor for `next` and the
wrong one for `curr`.**

The both-inside reading — ROUND-177's words, "where both are loop-local":

```sm
mukhya() {
    chakra i = 1 tak 3 {
        kosh curr[2]
        kosh next[2]
        yadi i == 1 { kosh_kship(curr, 10) }
        likha(curr[0])
        kosh_kship(next, i * 10)
        curr = next
    }
}
```

Pass 1 (both hoisted, scan whitelisting `curr = next` for both candidates):
`curr` decl → fresh D1; seed D1 = [10]; `likha(curr[0])` = 10. `next` decl
→ fresh D2 = [10]. Swap → `curr` ← D2, `next` ← D1.
Pass 2: `curr` decl → slot holds D2 → **reset `[D2-16]` = 0 → `curr` is
EMPTY**. `likha(curr[0])` → bounds check fails → **exit 1** (it should print
10, the carried value).

And without the hoist (current v319, no scan change) the same program fails
the same way: pass 2's `curr` decl allocates a FRESH empty descriptor, so
`likha(curr[0])` exits 1 there too. **The both-inside pattern is broken by
the language itself — a `kosh` declared inside a loop is fresh each pass —
not by the ping-pong.** The ping-pong presupposes a carried value that the
decl destroys, and it cannot fix that, because the swap and the reset are in
the wrong order for `curr`: the value is carried at the end of pass N and
destroyed at the top of pass N+1, before any read.

The only way the both-inside reading works is a second, undescribed change:
a "carried" decl mode in which `curr`'s decl does NOT reset the length — i.e.
two hoist modes, "emptied" and "carried", with the scan designating which
name is carried. The proposal does not describe it, and even with it, §2.2
and §2.3 still break (they are about the recycled descriptor, not the carried
one). **The loop-carried pattern, as the language stands, needs `curr`
declared OUTSIDE the loop** — and there it needs the scan change of §2.1.

The stale-elements half of the question is settled by the read path (§2.0): a
kosh read bounds-checks against the length freshly read from the descriptor,
so stale elements are invisible once the length is 0. The danger is the
LENGTH, not the elements: between the swap and the next decl, the recycled
descriptor D1 still carries its old length, so its old elements are readable
through `next` (§2.2) and through any alias (§2.3).

### 2.8 Case 6 — growth across the swap: CLOSED

Growth operates through the descriptor, never through the slot. After the
swap, `next`'s slot holds D1, so a push into `next` grows D1 via its `[d]`
and `[d+8]` — in place if the new size fits the region, otherwise a new
buffer, an element-by-element copy, a munmap of the old region, and the three
words `[r8]`, `[r8-8]`, `[r8+8]` written together (`r127_emit_grow`,
`src/r65_arena.inc:579-745`). Every name holding D1 sees the new buffer,
because no name holds the elements pointer directly. The swap itself releases
nothing and leaks nothing: D1's region stays live as long as D1 is reachable
(via `next`'s slot), and when the loop ends D1 is still live (it is `next`'s
last value) and D2 is still live (it is `curr`'s last value) — the normal "no
`kosh` ever dies" state, no worse than today.

Two details the implementer must preserve (not breaks):

- The R82 generation stamp: `r172_decl_begin` (v319) stamps the current
  block generation into `[rcx-24]` at the decl, so a recycled descriptor is
  re-stamped every pass. The swap must not skip that stamp, and it does not
  need to write one itself — the decl does it. (`[rcx-32]`, the block-slot
  address, is NOT re-stamped by the decl; it still points at the block where
  the descriptor was created. The liveness check (`r82_emit_check`, `:11531`)
  reads `[rax-32]` then `[rax-24]` and compares the generation at that slot
  with the stamped one — it runs only on `kosh` parameters in a `prakriya`
  prologue, and a hoisted descriptor never becomes a parameter (the scan
  flags the call), so the stale `[d-32]` is not reachable through the check
  as the design stands. If the ping-pong ever let a swapped descriptor reach
  a parameter, the check would compare a generation against a descriptor
  pointer and fail falsely — a second reason the swap must stay behind the
  scan.)
- A pass where `next` grows past 4,097 elements (region-backed) and then the
  swap recycles the SMALL D1 into `next`'s slot: the next pass grows D1
  again — first in place at the arena cursor / a new arena allocation, then
  past 4,097 a new region, copy, munmap of D1's old (arena, not a region —
  nothing to munmap). Correct; the region is released when outgrown, not at
  the swap.

### 2.9 Instruction count — not the same as the detach

Detach (ROUND-177 WS1), 3 instructions, 1 register:

```
mov  rax, [rbp - temp_slot]
mov  [rbp - best_slot], rax     ; best now owns that descriptor
mov  QWORD [rbp - temp_slot], 0 ; DETACH
```

Ping-pong, plain moves — 4 instructions, 2 registers:

```
mov  rax, [rbp - curr_slot]    ; rax = D1 (old curr)
mov  rcx, [rbp - next_slot]    ; rcx = D2 (this pass's next)
mov  [rbp - next_slot], rax    ; next = D1
mov  [rbp - curr_slot], rcx    ; curr = D2
```

A swap of two memory locations needs a temporary and at least four memory
operations; the detach needs three because one side is a constant (0). With
a length reset on the recycled descriptor it is 5 (`mov qword [rax-16],0`);
with a zero check around that reset it is 7. The only 3-instruction form is:

```
mov  rax, [rbp - curr_slot]
xchg rax, [rbp - next_slot]    ; locked: an implicit LOCK prefix
mov  [rbp - curr_slot], rax
```

— the same COUNT as the detach, but `xchg` with a memory operand is
implicitly locked, so it costs more than three plain moves (a locked
operation serialises). **So no: not the same instruction count with plain
moves, and not the same cost with `xchg`.**

One more implementation wrinkle: the assignment has MANY emitters
(`.gs_assign`'s PERF1/PERF2/XMM/float fast paths, all returning early at
`.gs_assign_fast_done`). The swap would have to hook every path that can emit
a kosh-to-kosh assignment, or run before them in `.gs_assign`'s prologue
(where `r126_check_assign` and `r38_mark_alias` already run for var-var
assignments). A single hook point exists, but it is a hook the proposal does
not name.

**Ranked breaking programs for B (strongest first):**

1. **§2.3 — three names** (`prev = curr` before the swap): the recycled
   descriptor is aliased by a third name. Plain semantics exits 1;
   ping-pong prints 20. The ping-pong does not know about the third name —
   the alias machinery records only the KIND, never which descriptor a name
   holds, and there is no table in the compiler that says "D1 is held by
   `prev`".
2. **§2.2 — `next` read after the swap**: `next`'s slot holds the old `curr`
   descriptor D1 with its old length and old elements, until the next pass's
   decl resets it. `likha(next[0])` on pass 2 prints 10 instead of 20 — a
   stale read (unguarded, it exits 1 on pass 1 instead of printing 10).
3. **§2.7 — the both-inside reading**: the proposal's stated shape — "both
   are loop-local" (ROUND-177 WS1 Q2) — cannot carry a value at all. A `kosh`
   declared inside the loop is fresh each pass; if hoisted, its decl resets
   the carried descriptor's length to 0 at the top of the next pass, before
   any read. The pattern only makes sense with `curr` declared OUTSIDE the
   loop — and there it needs a scan change the proposal does not describe.

**Closed:** §2.4 before the swap (any escape of `next` prevents hoisting, so
the swap never fires); §2.5 empty `next` (capacities travel with the
descriptors, `curr` correctly empty); §2.8 growth across the swap (growth
operates entirely through the descriptor). §2.6 zero slot is closed for the
plain pointer swap (correct, wasteful — it is exactly what the detach would
write), but a length-resetting swap would segfault on it, so the two
mitigations conflict.

---

## 3. Task 3 — C: move the zeroing from `mandapa` exit into `nirmmita`. No.

The proposal (ROUND-165 §1.C / ROUND-177 WS2): leave `mandapa` exit
untouched — one cursor restore, exactly as v315 — and zero inside `nirmmita`
instead. Argument: a `kosh` cannot read stale bytes (length check), a `mala`
zeroes at creation, so only `nirmmita` can observe stale data. Claim: this
restores all 38 guard programs to byte-identical and takes block exit from
13.2 ns back to 5.6. The WS2 design adds: zero only when the allocation is
below the block's mark — a `nirmmita` into virgin pages needs nothing, because
the kernel already zeroed them.

The claim fails on its central premise and on both of its stated
consequences. (And §0 first: the zeroing it would move is not in v319 at
all — C is a proposal, not a move of existing code.)

### 3.1 Q1 — does a `kosh` read ever look past `length`? No, through the language's own read paths. Yes, through FFI.

`k[i]` (read) and `k[i] = v` (write) both go through the same sequence
(`src/sutram_compiler.asm`, v318):

- `gen_expr` evaluates the runtime index → `rax`.
- `r128_emit_kosh_refresh` (`:21719`) re-reads the length and the elements
  from the descriptor **after** the index expression, when the index contains
  a call (`r12 = 0x102` — a call in the index may have grown the `kosh`, so
  the length is read again, not the one from before the call).
- `emit_array_bounds_check` (`:21588`) emits `cmp rax, [rsp]` — the unsigned
  index against the runtime length on the stack — and `jb .ok`; otherwise it
  prints the diagnostic and exits 1. Unsigned, so a negative index fails too.

The check is emitted whenever `r12 != 0`, and for a `kosh`
`r128_emit_kosh_base` sets `r12 = 2` (or `0x102` when the index has a call) —
never 0. There is no constant-index exemption: a constant index cannot be
proven `< length` at compile time, because `length` is runtime, so the check
is emitted for constant indices too. The write path (`:22397-22400`) is
identical.

Slice creation (`r23_gen_slice`, `src/r23_capabilities.inc:574`) emits two
runtime checks via `emit_array_bounds_check`: `hi <= length` (`hi < length +
1`, unsigned, so a negative `hi` fails too; the length is re-read after `lo`
and `hi` are evaluated, and an omitted `hi` takes the length after `lo` was
evaluated, not before — R129) and `lo <= hi` (`lo < hi + 1`). Reads through
the slice (`r23_index_slice`) push the slice's length and check against it
(the `r12 = 3` path in the index handler). A slice of a `mala` is likewise
checked against the `mala`'s stored length. A whole `mala` passed to a
`vibhag` parameter becomes a slice `[0:length]` (`.r23_gtca_slice`, `:27833`),
and reads through it are checked against that length.

The other `kosh` readers are all bounded by `length`: `kosh_iksh` (search),
`kosh_yuj` (concat), the growth copy (`r127_emit_grow`) — all iterate
`[0, length)`. `kosh_gan` reads `[d-16]`; `kosh_ksham` reads `[d-8]`;
`parimana()` reads capacity; the R82 liveness check reads the header words
`[d-40]`, `[d-32]`, `[d-24]`. None reads past `length`. `likha(k)` prints the
descriptor address (R100 trap #5, still live) — it hands the address to the
output, not to the program.

**The one exception: FFI.** A foreign function taking a `ptr` parameter
receives the elements address (`src/r32_ffi.inc:736-755`, `.ptr` emits
`mov rax,[rax]`), and C can read — and write — past `length`. A foreign
function can also return the address (`ptr` / `i64` result kinds,
`src/r32_ffi.inc:15-16`, `:297-298`), and the program can then read through
it with `akshar` (raw byte read, no bounds check — R163 Task 3, `:24405`) or
pointer arithmetic. This is the R162 hole's cousin: the bounds check is a
Sutram-side check, and C is outside it. So the claim "a `kosh` cannot read
